<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Package sources for this solution. <clear /> drops any inherited sources from
    machine-level NuGet.Config so the build is reproducible from this file alone.
    Directory.Build.props turns on RestorePackagesWithLockFile, and without this
    file the committed packages.lock.json files claimed a reproducibility they
    could not deliver: they resolved against whatever machine-level configuration
    happened to be present. On the machine this was written on that was two
    sources rather than one - nuget.org and a "Microsoft Visual Studio Offline
    Packages" folder under Program Files - and a different clone need not have
    the second.

    Deliberately no `github` source and no packageSourceCredentials block. This
    repository consumes no HalHeinrich.* package: its BigRational edge is a
    ProjectReference, not a PackageReference, so the GITHUB_PACKAGES_PAT the
    consuming members need would be a restore failure mode here with nothing
    behind it. Only Collatz.Tests restores from a feed at all - the xUnit,
    test-SDK and coverage tooling, all of it public - while Collatz and
    Collatz.Experiments carry no PackageReference. Add the source back only if a
    HalHeinrich.* package is ever referenced from this repo.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Deliberately no packageSourceMapping, which is where this file departs from
    the other members' copies. It is not omitted by oversight and should not be
    added to match them.

    With one declared source it would be inert for every package this repo
    restores: mapping `*` to nuget.org selects exactly the resolution no mapping
    at all produces. Its only effect would arrive on the day a second source is
    added, and measured on SDK 10.0.400 that effect points the wrong way. With a
    mapping present NuGet considers only the sources whose patterns match a given
    package id and names the rest as "not considered" in the restore error - so a
    `*` mapped to nuget.org would exclude a newly added github feed from every
    package, the HalHeinrich.* one that motivated adding the feed included. The
    private name would then be sought on the public feed and nowhere else, which
    is the direction dependency confusion runs rather than a defence against it.

    The defence that does work cannot be written in advance, because it has to
    name the source it protects: when a HalHeinrich.* PackageReference is added,
    add the github source above and, in that same change, a packageSourceMapping
    section mapping `HalHeinrich.*` to github and `*` to nuget.org.
  -->

</configuration>
